"""Concise art-direction decision; the linked standard holds the review protocol."""
from html import escape


def palette(colours):
    return '<span class="cast-swatches" aria-hidden="true">' + ''.join(
        f'<i style="background:{colour}"></i>' for colour in colours) + '</span>'


def render_readability():
    cast = [
        ('Worker 01', '1.00×', ['#335E8A', '#467BA4', '#6A94B5'], 'Industrial blue, several shades.',
         'Paint the broad armour plates; keep joints dark. The smallest, simplest bodies form a recognizable workforce.'),
        ('LIMEN', '1.25×', ['#AAB9BD', '#39434A', '#39D8DF'], 'Light steel. One cyan blade.',
         'Lift the value of his broad armour. Breadth and the asymmetric emitter must identify him even with the blade off.'),
        ('STILETTO', '1.20×', ['#24292E', '#C5443C', '#C6D0D3'], 'Black chrome. Red body accents.',
         'Try red on shoulders and upper back, keeping most of her body black. Long limbs and twin tools preserve her silhouette with blades or welders.'),
        ('CATHEXIS', '1.22×', ['#D8CBAF', '#A98A52', '#3B3937'], 'Aged ivory. Gold. A pale skirt.',
         'Preserve her approved dress, crown and hair. Increase her scale; keep enough pale fabric visible in dirty states to retain her identity.'),
    ]
    rows = ''.join(f'''<article class="cast-direction-row">
        <div><h4>{escape(name)}</h4><span class="cast-size">{size} body height</span></div>
        <div>{palette(colours)}<strong>{title}</strong><p>{change}</p></div>
        </article>''' for name, size, colours, title, change in cast)
    return '<div class="cast-refinement"><h3 id="cast-and-room-readability">Cast and room readability</h3><h3 id="preserve-the-approved-identities">Preserve the approved identities</h3><p>Workers remain a family of worn industrial blues with dark joints and distinct head and body shapes. bob keeps the small, flat head under oversized cyan fly-eye lenses, a narrow frame and articulated hands. LIMEN keeps his broad steel body and cyan blade identity. STILETTO keeps black chrome, red accents, long limbs and twin-tool silhouette. CATHEXIS keeps the approved 100% ivory paint strength and 1.3× worker neutral-body height, with dress, crown and hair. The 19 October owner decision supersedes the earlier 1.22× proposal; she was not assessed in the captured Day1 slice. Do not use a shared generic worker shape or a text label as a substitute for these differences.</p><p>The earlier comparison used worker body height as 1.00, with LIMEN 1.25, STILETTO 1.20 and CATHEXIS 1.22. Those are historical comparison settings, not a new instruction to rescale accepted models. Tool and crown extents remain separate from body height. Preserve the approved source references and the current model review links.</p><h3 id="compose-the-room-around-the-people">Compose the room around the people</h3><p>The selected pale floor, quiet walking lanes and dark machinery give the cast useful separation. Keep the original Lobby, Security and Conveyor identities: entrance and admission portals, desks, pipes, the connected machine line, and each real control. Concept paintings establish the factory&#x27;s materials and history; their dense darkness need not be copied into every operating shot. No brighter global glow or additional floor detail is required by the current review.</p><p>Actual R10 screenshots show the blue workforce and black/red STILETTO separating from the floor in wide Conveyor views. They also show heads hidden by dialogue and credential cards. Fix the obstruction before judging the source character design. The current review does not establish a timed names-hidden recognition result for every worker, nor a Day 1 performance result for CATHEXIS.</p><figure><a href="/loopforge-dev-blog/media/2026-10-25-day-one/r10-conveyor-no-speech.png"><img src="/loopforge-dev-blog/media/2026-10-25-day-one/r10-conveyor-no-speech.png" width="1280" height="720" alt="Blue workers and black/red STILETTO on the pale Conveyor floor." loading="lazy" decoding="async"></a><figcaption>Published R10, 08:06:29. Blue workers and black/red STILETTO separate from the pale floor and dark machinery in a no-speech view. This is not a names-hidden recognition test. Actual running-game captures: Producer-operated studio browser; departure frame extracted from that recording by Animator. Existing project art credits remain.</figcaption></figure><h3 id="review-the-performed-relationship">Review the performed relationship</h3><p>Judge the actor, recipient, control or workpiece and visible consequence together. A close-up asset approval does not certify a crowded shot. Use the selected stable picture and camera phases for the first integrated comparison, then check the same moment with speech, a reaction and silence. Preserve useful silhouettes when optional effects are reduced.</p><p>The next evidence must show real corrected desktop and narrow views. One still can prove that a head is visible; it cannot certify fluidity, contact timing or a whole shift. Keep the existing cast readability studio and full visual standard as review tools, alongside the actual Day 1 scene.</p><details><summary>Historical palette and scale comparisons — not model-change instructions</summary><p>These earlier swatches and numeric comparisons remain reference material. They do not authorize resizing or repainting accepted bodies.</p><div class="cast-room-palette">' + palette(['#666860', '#343D38']) + '<span>Earlier floor and wall comparison swatches</span></div><div class="cast-directions">' + rows + '</div></details><p><a href="/stepanoskin/loopforge/play/readability" target="_top">Open the static readability studio →</a></p><div class="cast-reference-links"><a href="/loopforge-design/production/CAST_SHEET_REVISION_BRIEF.md">Sheet revision brief ↗</a><a href="/loopforge-design/production/CAST_READABILITY_STANDARD.md">Full visual standard &amp; review checks ↗</a></div></div>'
